//@version=6
indicator(
     "Swing Volume Profile", shorttitle="SWINGVP", overlay=true,
     max_boxes_count=500, max_lines_count=500, max_labels_count=500,
     max_bars_back=1000
     )

// max_bars_back is REQUIRED, not decorative: every profile scan reads
// high[i]/low[i]/volume[i] with a LOOP VARIABLE, and Pine cannot infer the
// buffer depth from a dynamic index. The scan is capped by Max Bars Per Swing
// so the buffer is always sufficient.

// A horizontal volume profile built PER SWING rather than over a fixed window.
// Swings are found automatically from pivots, so the profile always describes
// the leg price is actually in — not an arbitrary last-N-bars box.
//
// Each swing gets five reference lines and a volume tag:
//   • POC       — the price row that traded the most total volume
//   • Max Buy   — the row with the most estimated BUYING
//   • Max Sell  — the row with the most estimated SELLING
//   • VAH / VAL — the edges of the value area, the band holding 70% of the
//                 swing's volume. Its EDGES are what people trade: price
//                 leaving the area is the market disagreeing with the price it
//                 spent the whole swing agreeing on.
//   • a label above each profile with the swing's total volume and buy share
//
// And ONE line that answers a question rather than describing history:
//
//   • ENTRY — the strongest volume IMBALANCE on the tradeable side of price.
//     For a buy, the row below price where buying most outweighed selling; for
//     a sell, the row above price where selling most outweighed buying. Scored
//     as (buy - sell) / busiest row, which is one number carrying both things
//     that matter: how lopsided the row was, scaled by how much volume it held.
//     A row where buyers barely won on tiny volume scores near zero, and so
//     does a huge row that was evenly matched. Neither is a level. What scores
//     high is a price where one side traded heavily and the other did not
//     answer.
//
//     IT IS A LEVEL, NOT A SIGNAL. It says where the book was heavy. It says
//     nothing about trend, direction, or whether now is the time. Below the
//     Minimum Strength input nothing is drawn at all, because a profile with no
//     strong imbalance genuinely has no level to offer and inventing one is the
//     exact failure this indicator should not have.
//
// Tuned for the 5-minute chart out of the box: Pivot Length 10 (≈50-minute
// swings), 30 rows, 3 swings. Those are inputs — raise Pivot Length on a lower
// timeframe, lower it on a higher one.
//
// HOW BUY/SELL IS ESTIMATED — the honest part:
//   Pine exposes no bid/ask, so no script can know which side was the
//   aggressor. Each bar's volume is split by where it CLOSED inside its own
//   range: a bar closing at its high counts fully as buying, at its low fully
//   as selling, in the middle 50/50.
//
//     buyShare = (close - low) / (high - low)
//
//   That is the standard approximation for a profile of this kind. It is not
//   order flow. It is right most of the time and wrong on bars that reverse
//   hard intrabar — which is exactly where you would most want it to be right.
//
//   The alternative, request.security_lower_tf(), was deliberately NOT used
//   here: on a 5-minute chart the finest intrabar resolution available on a
//   non-Premium plan is 1 minute, which is five sub-bars per candle — barely
//   better than this and far more expensive. See indicators/volume_pro for the
//   intrabar approach where it genuinely pays off.
//
// RESOLUTION: Price Rows Per Swing goes to 500. The rows are always BUILT at
//   the number you ask for, so the three marker lines get more precise with every
//   row you add — that is arrays, not drawings, and it is not rationed. What is
//   rationed is the drawing: TradingView allows 500 boxes for the whole script.
//   Rows holding no volume cost no box, the newest swing draws first, and the
//   table reports the budget in use. To buy more drawn detail: fewer swings, or
//   turn the sell|buy split off, which halves the cost per row.
//
// DOES IT ACTUALLY WORK? The indicator answers that itself. Every level it
//   would have drawn is recorded when its swing closes, then followed: when
//   price reaches the level, did the move that followed travel the target
//   distance in the level's favour before travelling the same distance against?
//   The table reports the share that did, with the sample size.
//
//   This is NOT a backtest. It ignores spread, commission and slippage, it uses
//   one symmetric target and stop, and it says nothing about sizing or exits.
//   When a single bar covers both the target and the stop, the order they
//   happened in is unknowable from bar data and it is counted as a LOSS —
//   calling it a win would inflate every number in the block. What it does tell
//   you is whether these levels hold on THIS symbol and timeframe more often
//   than not, which no strength percentage can. Under ~20 samples it is noise.
//
// SWING SOURCE: three ways to define a leg, all feeding the same pipeline.
//   Pivots on the chart timeframe (default), pivots on a higher timeframe, or
//   trading sessions. Sessions are exact. The higher-timeframe pivot's PRICE is
//   exact but its BAR is converted by the timeframe ratio, so across gaps the
//   left edge can land a bar or two off — stated in the input tooltip too,
//   because a user choosing it deserves to know before they choose.
//
// CONFLUENCE: when levels from different swings land within a few ticks of each
//   other, they are one zone, not three lines. The agreement is the
//   information, and separate lines hide it.
//
// ABSORPTION: a swing that makes a new extreme while the OTHER side's share of
//   its volume rises. The extreme says one side pushed; the rising share says
//   the other side was there to take it. Both halves are required — a new low
//   on rising selling is just a downtrend.
//
// ACCURACY: intrabar first, models only where intrabar cannot reach.
//   Use Intrabar Data is the largest single gain available here. Without it, a
//   bar's volume must be SPREAD across every price it touched using a guess
//   about where inside the bar the trading happened. With it, each sub-bar is
//   placed at its own narrow range carrying its own close position, so the
//   profile is largely measured. The table's Data row says what share of the
//   shown swing was built that way; TradingView's intrabar budget is finite and
//   older history falls back to the model, per bar, with nothing breaking.
//
//   SECONDS TIMEFRAMES NEED PREMIUM. A seconds value in Intrabar Timeframe
//   fails the WHOLE script on a lower plan, not just this feature. The default
//   is one minute for that reason.
//
// STRENGTH BUBBLES: a green dot under a bar where buying overwhelmed selling,
//   a red one where selling overwhelmed buying. Strength means the BAR'S OWN
//   DELTA, measured with the same intrabar data the profile is built from — not
//   candle size, not candle colour. A big green candle on thin, evenly-matched
//   volume gets no bubble, and it should not: nothing was overwhelmed.
//
//   Two floors, as everywhere else here. How one-sided the bar was, AND how
//   much volume it carried against its own average. Either alone lies: a bar
//   can be 90% buying on almost no volume, which is an empty book rather than
//   strength, and a bar can carry huge volume that was evenly matched, which is
//   a fight rather than a win.
//
//   The bar's direction has to agree too. A bar with heavy BUY delta that still
//   closed DOWN gets no green bubble — that is buying being absorbed, which
//   this script reports separately, and marking it green would contradict that.
//   The shade of each bubble carries how far past the threshold it went.
//
// LOW VOLUME NODES: the rows almost nothing traded in, tinted separately. They
//   are the opposite of the POC and just as informative — price accelerates
//   through them because there is nothing there to trade against, which makes
//   them poor targets and poor places to stand. They cost no extra drawings.
//
// NAKED POC: the most-agreed price of a finished leg that price has not come
//   back to. VALUE MIGRATION: where the POC went from one finished leg to the
//   next, which is the cheapest read of control there is.
//
// Below the intrabar path, two models, neither pretending to be order flow.
//   Distribution — a bar's volume is spread across the rows it spans. Even
//     gives a wick the same volume per row as the body, which is wrong in an
//     obvious way: a wick is a price the market REJECTED and traded through
//     quickly. Body-weighted is the default and puts most of the volume between
//     open and close.
//   Buy/sell split — see above. The plain close-in-range model is the default;
//     the blend also counts bar direction, which helps on trending bars and
//     hurts on choppy ones. Neither is verifiably better everywhere, so the
//     default is the well-understood one.
//
// PERFORMANCE: three tiers, because they change at three different rates.
//   A completed swing's profile is computed ONCE, when the swing closes.
//   The developing swing is recomputed once per BAR, not per tick.
//   The profile drawing — up to 492 box .set_*() calls — now also runs once per
//     bar. It used to run on every tick, redrawing a picture that was already
//     correct, and it was by far the most expensive thing this script did.
//   Only the entry level tracks live price, so only it runs per tick: one pass
//     over the rows plus two line updates.
// See references/performance-guide.md.

// ————— Constants
color TRANSPARENT_COLOR = #00000000
string THEME_DARK       = "Dark"
string THEME_LIGHT      = "Light"
int    MARKERS_PER_SWING = 5          // POC, max buy, max sell, VAH, VAL
int    MAX_ROW_BOXES     = 484        // 500 box cap, 8 reserved for confluence
int    STAT_ATR_LEN      = 14         // the conventional ATR, so the number is comparable
string ENTRY_BEST  = "Best Side Only"
string ENTRY_BOTH  = "Both Sides"
string ENTRY_BUY   = "Buy Side Only"
string ENTRY_SELL  = "Sell Side Only"
string DIST_BODY   = "Body-weighted"
string DIST_EVEN   = "Even"
string SPLIT_RANGE = "Close position in range"
string SPLIT_BLEND = "Range + bar direction"
string SRC_PIVOT   = "Pivots (chart timeframe)"
string SRC_HTF     = "Pivots (higher timeframe)"
string SRC_SESSION = "Sessions"
// How much of a body row's volume a WICK row gets, under body-weighted
// distribution. A heuristic, not a measurement: Pine cannot say how volume
// was really spread inside a bar. What is not a heuristic is that giving a
// rejected price the same weight as a traded-through one is wrong.
float  WICK_WEIGHT  = 0.35

// ————— Inputs: Swing Detection
string SW_GROUP = "Swing Detection"
string swingSourceInput = input.string(
     SRC_PIVOT, "Swing Source", options=[SRC_PIVOT, SRC_HTF, SRC_SESSION],
     group=SW_GROUP,
     tooltip="What defines one leg.\n\nPivots (chart timeframe) is the default: " +
     "swing highs and lows on the chart you are looking at.\n\nPivots (higher " +
     "timeframe) finds the structure of a bigger chart while you trade the small " +
     "one. Read the caveat on the Higher Timeframe input — the leg boundary is " +
     "converted, not exact.\n\nSessions splits by trading session instead of by " +
     "structure, which is exact and is how most volume traders actually think " +
     "about gold: the London open regularly breaks the value Asia built.")
string htfInput = input.timeframe(
     "60", "Higher Timeframe", group=SW_GROUP,
     tooltip="Only used by the higher-timeframe source. HONEST CAVEAT: the pivot's " +
     "PRICE comes from that timeframe exactly, but its BAR is converted by the " +
     "ratio of the two timeframes, so across session gaps and holidays the left " +
     "edge of a leg can land a bar or two off. The profile it builds is still the " +
     "right leg; its boundary is approximate. Sessions mode has no such caveat.")
string sessionInput = input.session(
     "0000-0800", "Session", group=SW_GROUP,
     tooltip="Only used by the Sessions source. Each session becomes one profile. " +
     "Set it to the session you care about — Asia 0000-0800, London 0800-1600, " +
     "New York 1300-2100 (exchange time).")
int  pivotLenInput = input.int(
     10, "Pivot Length", minval=2, maxval=200, group=SW_GROUP,
     tooltip="How many bars either side a high/low must lead to count as a swing " +
     "point. 10 suits a 5-minute chart (~50-minute swings). Raise it for fewer, " +
     "bigger swings; lower it for more, smaller ones.")
int  maxSwingsInput = input.int(
     3, "Swings To Show", minval=1, maxval=6, group=SW_GROUP)
int  maxSwingBarsInput = input.int(
     300, "Max Bars Per Swing", minval=20, maxval=900, group=SW_GROUP,
     tooltip="Safety cap. A swing longer than this is scanned only over its most " +
     "recent bars, so one runaway leg cannot stall the script.")
bool showLiveSwingInput = input.bool(
     true, "Include The Developing Swing", group=SW_GROUP,
     tooltip="Profiles the leg from the last confirmed pivot to the current bar. " +
     "It updates as the leg extends, and its shape will keep changing until the " +
     "next pivot confirms.")

// ————— Inputs: Profile
string PR_GROUP = "Profile"
int    rowsInput = input.int(
     30, "Price Rows Per Swing", minval=5, maxval=500, step=5, group=PR_GROUP,
     tooltip="Resolution of the profile. Raising it always sharpens POC / Max Buy / " +
     "Max Sell, because those are computed at full resolution whether or not every " +
     "row gets drawn. Drawing is what is limited: TradingView allows 500 boxes for " +
     "the whole script, so boxes are spent on rows that actually hold volume, newest " +
     "swing first, and the table reports the budget in use. Rows are never finer " +
     "than one tick.")
string profilePosInput = input.string(
     "Over Swing", "Profile Position",
     options=["Over Swing", "Right Of Swing"], group=PR_GROUP)
int    profileWidthInput = input.int(
     40, "Width (bars, when right of swing)", minval=5, maxval=200, group=PR_GROUP)
float  maxWidthPctInput = input.float(
     70.0, "Max Width (% of swing)", minval=10.0, maxval=100.0, step=5.0, group=PR_GROUP,
     tooltip="When drawn over the swing, the longest row spans this share of the " +
     "swing's width, so the profile never buries the candles.")
int    rowTranspInput = input.int(
     78, "Row Transparency", minval=40, maxval=97, group=PR_GROUP)
bool   showSplitInput = input.bool(
     true, "Split Each Row Into Sell | Buy", group=PR_GROUP,
     tooltip="Draws the selling share and the buying share of each row as two " +
     "segments. Off draws one bar coloured by whichever side dominated - and costs " +
     "HALF the boxes, which is the cheapest way to buy more rows.")

// ————— Inputs: Entry Levels
string EN_GROUP = "Entry Levels"
bool   showEntryInput = input.bool(
     true, "Show Entry Level", group=EN_GROUP,
     tooltip="Marks the strongest volume IMBALANCE on the tradeable side of price: " +
     "for a buy, the row below price where buying most outweighed selling; for a " +
     "sell, the row above price where selling most outweighed buying. Those are the " +
     "prices one side previously defended and the other side failed to break." +
     "\n\nIt is a level, not a signal. It says where the book was heavy, not that " +
     "the trade is good, and it says nothing at all about direction, trend or " +
     "timing. Read Strength in the table before using it: a level built on a weak " +
     "imbalance is a level that will not hold.")
string entryModeInput = input.string(
     ENTRY_BEST, "Which Side", options=[ENTRY_BEST, ENTRY_BOTH, ENTRY_BUY, ENTRY_SELL],
     group=EN_GROUP,
     tooltip="Best Side draws only the stronger of the two, which is the one the " +
     "profile actually argues for. Both Sides draws the buy level below price and " +
     "the sell level above it at the same time — useful for framing a range, " +
     "cluttered when you only want one decision.")
float  entryMinVolumeInput = input.float(
     30.0, "Minimum Row Volume (%)", minval=0.0, maxval=90.0, step=5.0, group=EN_GROUP,
     tooltip="An entry row must hold at least this share of the busiest row's " +
     "volume. Without it a level can land on a thin shelf that happened to be " +
     "one-sided — lopsided because almost nothing traded there, not because one " +
     "side was overwhelmed. Price does not defend a price nobody was at." +
     "\n\nThis is a floor on SIZE; Minimum Strength below is a floor on how " +
     "one-sided it was. A level needs both.")
float  entryMinStrengthInput = input.float(
     15.0, "Minimum Strength (%)", minval=1.0, maxval=100.0, step=1.0, group=EN_GROUP,
     tooltip="How lopsided a row must be before it counts, as a percentage of the " +
     "busiest row in the swing. Below this nothing is drawn and the table says so — " +
     "a profile with no strong imbalance genuinely has no level to offer, and " +
     "inventing one would be the failure mode this input exists to prevent.")
bool   entryExtendInput = input.bool(
     true, "Extend Entry Lines Right", group=EN_GROUP)
int    entryWidthInput = input.int(
     2, "Entry Line Width", minval=1, maxval=4, group=EN_GROUP)
string entrySourceInput = input.string(
     "All shown swings", "Search For Levels In",
     options=["All shown swings", "Newest swing only"], group=EN_GROUP,
     tooltip="The strongest level available is often in an OLDER leg that price " +
     "has not come back to yet — an untested imbalance is more interesting than a " +
     "tested one, not less. Newest swing only restricts the search to the leg " +
     "price is in now, which is fresher but has less to work with early in a " +
     "swing.\n\nStrength is measured against each swing's own busiest row, so " +
     "levels from different swings stay comparable.")
bool   preferUntestedInput = input.bool(
     true, "Only Untested Levels", group=EN_GROUP,
     tooltip="Skips rows price has already traded back through since the swing " +
     "ended. An imbalance that has been revisited has already had its argument " +
     "tested; one that has not is still standing. The developing swing is never " +
     "filtered, because price is inside it by definition.")
string alertFormatInput = input.string(
     "Text", "Alert Format", options=["Text", "JSON (webhook)"], group=EN_GROUP,
     tooltip="JSON emits a flat object a bot can parse directly — symbol, " +
     "timeframe, side, price, strength and bar time. See " +
     "references/alerts-guide.md. Do not put anything private in an alert: the " +
     "payload leaves TradingView to whatever URL you configure.")
bool   entryAlertInput = input.bool(
     true, "Alert When Price Reaches The Level", group=EN_GROUP,
     tooltip="Fires on bar close, once per bar. Mid-bar the touch can un-happen, " +
     "and an alert for something that never finished is worse than no alert.")

// ————— Inputs: Strength Bubbles
string BB_GROUP = "Strength Bubbles"
bool   showBubblesInput = input.bool(
     true, "Show Strength Bubbles", group=BB_GROUP,
     tooltip="Marks the bars where one side genuinely overwhelmed the other: a " +
     "green bubble under a strong up bar, a red one for a strong down bar." +
     "\n\nStrength here means the BAR'S OWN DELTA, measured with the same " +
     "intrabar data the profile is built from — not candle size, not a colour. " +
     "A big green candle on thin, evenly-matched volume gets no bubble, and it " +
     "should not: nothing was overwhelmed.")
float  bubbleDeltaInput = input.float(
     40.0, "Minimum Delta (%)", minval=5.0, maxval=95.0, step=5.0, group=BB_GROUP,
     tooltip="How one-sided the bar's volume must be. 40 means buying exceeded " +
     "selling by 40% of the bar's total volume.")
float  bubbleVolInput = input.float(
     1.3, "Minimum Volume (x average)", minval=0.5, maxval=10.0, step=0.1, group=BB_GROUP,
     tooltip="And how much volume it must carry, against its own recent average. " +
     "This is the second floor, and it is the one that matters: a bar can be 90% " +
     "buying on almost no volume. That is not strength, it is an empty book.")
int    bubbleVolLenInput = input.int(
     20, "Volume Average Length", minval=2, maxval=200, group=BB_GROUP)
string bubblePosInput = input.string(
     "Below bars", "Bubble Position",
     options=["Below bars", "Bull below, bear above"], group=BB_GROUP,
     tooltip="Below bars keeps them in one readable row under price. The other " +
     "option puts each bubble on the side its direction points.")
color  bubbleBullColorInput = input.color(#089981, "Bull Bubble", inline="bb", group=BB_GROUP)
color  bubbleBearColorInput = input.color(#f23645, "Bear Bubble", inline="bb", group=BB_GROUP)
bool   bubbleAlertInput = input.bool(
     false, "Alert On Strength Bubbles", group=BB_GROUP,
     tooltip="Fires on bar close. Off by default because on a busy symbol these " +
     "are frequent.")

// ————— Inputs: Confluence
string CF_GROUP = "Confluence"
bool   showLvnInput = input.bool(
     true, "Highlight Low Volume Nodes", group=PR_GROUP,
     tooltip="Tints the rows almost nothing traded in. They are the opposite of " +
     "the POC and just as informative: price accelerates THROUGH a low volume " +
     "node because there is nothing there to trade against, so they make poor " +
     "targets and poor places to stand." +
     "\n\nCosts no extra drawings — the rows are already being drawn, this only " +
     "changes their colour.")
float  lvnPctInput = input.float(
     15.0, "Low Volume Node Below (%)", minval=2.0, maxval=50.0, step=1.0, group=PR_GROUP,
     tooltip="A row counts as a low volume node when it holds less than this " +
     "share of the busiest row's volume.")
color  lvnColorInput = input.color(#787b86, "Low Volume Node Color", group=PR_GROUP)

bool   showConfluenceInput = input.bool(
     true, "Merge Overlapping Levels Into Zones", group=CF_GROUP,
     tooltip="When the POC of one swing, the value-area edge of another and the " +
     "max-buy row of a third all land within a few ticks, that is one ZONE, not " +
     "three lines. Drawn as a band labelled with how many levels agree." +
     "\n\nThe agreement is the information. Three separate lines on a chart hide " +
     "the very thing that makes them worth looking at.")
float  confluenceAtrInput = input.float(
     0.25, "Merge Distance (ATR)", minval=0.05, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=CF_GROUP,
     tooltip="How close two levels must be to count as the same zone, in ATR so " +
     "it adapts to the symbol instead of assuming a tick size.")
int    confluenceMinInput = input.int(
     2, "Minimum Levels Per Zone", minval=2, maxval=6, group=CF_GROUP)
color  confluenceColorInput = input.color(#2962ff, "Zone Color", group=CF_GROUP)

// ————— Inputs: Value Area
string VA_GROUP = "Value Area"
bool   showValueAreaInput = input.bool(
     true, "Show Value Area (VAH / VAL)", group=VA_GROUP,
     tooltip="The price band containing the given share of the swing's volume, " +
     "grown outward from the POC. It is the range the market agreed on. Its edges " +
     "matter more than its middle: price leaving the value area is the market " +
     "disagreeing, and price returning into it is the disagreement failing.")
float  valueAreaPctInput = input.float(
     70.0, "Value Area (%)", minval=30.0, maxval=95.0, step=5.0, group=VA_GROUP,
     tooltip="70% is the convention, and conventions matter here because other " +
     "traders are drawing the same line.")
color  vaColorInput = input.color(#787b86, "Value Area Color", group=VA_GROUP)

// ————— Inputs: Accuracy
string AC_GROUP = "Accuracy"
bool   useIntrabarInput = input.bool(
     true, "Use Intrabar Data", group=AC_GROUP,
     tooltip="The largest accuracy gain available to this script, and the one " +
     "that makes the two models below matter less." +
     "\n\nWithout it, a bar's volume has to be SPREAD across every price it " +
     "touched, using a guess about where inside the bar the trading happened. " +
     "With it, each sub-bar is placed at its own narrow price range and carries " +
     "its own close position — so the profile is largely measured rather than " +
     "modelled, and the buy/sell split gets one estimate per sub-bar instead of " +
     "one per candle." +
     "\n\nIt falls back to the bar-level model, per bar, wherever TradingView " +
     "returns no intrabar data — which it will for older history, since the " +
     "intrabar budget is finite. Nothing breaks; the older part of the chart is " +
     "simply modelled the old way.")
string intrabarTfInput = input.timeframe(
     "1", "Intrabar Timeframe", group=AC_GROUP,
     tooltip="Must be LOWER than your chart timeframe or it is ignored. On a " +
     "5-minute chart, 1 minute gives five sub-bars per candle." +
     "\n\nSECONDS TIMEFRAMES REQUIRE A PREMIUM PLAN. Typing 15S here on a lower " +
     "plan makes the whole script fail to load, not just this feature. Leave it " +
     "on a minute value unless you know you have Premium.")
string distModeInput = input.string(
     DIST_BODY, "Volume Distribution", options=[DIST_BODY, DIST_EVEN], group=AC_GROUP,
     tooltip="How one bar's volume is spread across the price rows it spans. Even " +
     "gives a wick the same volume per row as the body, which is wrong in an " +
     "obvious way: a wick is a price the market REJECTED and traded through " +
     "quickly. Body-weighted puts most of the bar's volume between open and close " +
     "and is the better default.")
string splitModeInput = input.string(
     SPLIT_RANGE, "Buy/Sell Estimate", options=[SPLIT_RANGE, SPLIT_BLEND], group=AC_GROUP,
     tooltip="Pine exposes no bid/ask, so both of these are estimates and neither " +
     "is order flow. Close-in-range is the standard one. Range + direction also " +
     "counts whether the bar closed above or below the previous close, which helps " +
     "on trending bars and hurts on choppy ones. Neither is verifiably better " +
     "everywhere, which is why the default is left on the plain, well-understood " +
     "one rather than quietly changed under you.")
bool   pocAlertInput = input.bool(
     true, "Alert When Price Reaches A Naked POC", group=AC_GROUP,
     tooltip="A naked POC is a point of control price has not returned to since " +
     "the swing that built it ended. It is the single most-agreed price of that " +
     "leg, still unvisited.")
bool   showAbsorptionInput = input.bool(
     true, "Detect Absorption", group=AC_GROUP,
     tooltip="Flags the classic volume-analysis reversal shape: a swing that makes " +
     "a NEW EXTREME while the other side's share of its volume RISES. A lower low " +
     "on rising buy share means the selling that made the low was being absorbed — " +
     "someone was taking the other side and the price did not follow through." +
     "\n\nIt compares the newest completed swing with the one before it, so it " +
     "needs two. Like everything else here it uses estimated buy/sell, not order " +
     "flow.")
bool   showStatsInput = input.bool(
     true, "Measure Level Hit Rate", group=AC_GROUP,
     tooltip="Every level this indicator draws is recorded when its swing closes " +
     "and then followed. When price reaches it, the move that follows is measured: " +
     "did it travel the target distance in the level's favour before travelling " +
     "the same distance against? The table reports the share that did." +
     "\n\nTHIS IS NOT A BACKTEST. It ignores spread, commission and slippage, it " +
     "uses one symmetric target and stop, and it says nothing about position size " +
     "or when to exit. What it does tell you is whether these levels hold on THIS " +
     "symbol and timeframe more often than not — which is the question a strength " +
     "percentage alone cannot answer. Treat a sample under about 20 as noise.")
float  statTargetAtrInput = input.float(
     1.0, "Hit Rate Target (ATR)", minval=0.2, maxval=10.0, step=0.1, group=AC_GROUP,
     tooltip="How far price must travel from the level, in ATR, for the level to " +
     "count as having held — and equally far against it to count as broken. " +
     "Symmetric on purpose: an asymmetric test would flatter the result.")
int    statMaxLevelsInput = input.int(
     60, "Levels To Track", minval=10, maxval=200, group=AC_GROUP)
bool   showVolumeTagInput = input.bool(
     true, "Label Each Swing's Total Volume", group=AC_GROUP,
     tooltip="Prints the swing's total traded volume above its profile, with the " +
     "buy share. The developing swing is prefixed with ~ because its number is " +
     "still moving.")

// ————— Inputs: Marker Lines
string LN_GROUP = "Marker Lines"
bool   showPocInput     = input.bool(true, "POC (most total volume)", inline="poc", group=LN_GROUP)
color  pocColorInput    = input.color(#d98b1f, "", inline="poc", group=LN_GROUP)
bool   showMaxBuyInput  = input.bool(true, "Max Buy row", inline="mb", group=LN_GROUP)
color  maxBuyColorInput = input.color(#089981, "", inline="mb", group=LN_GROUP)
bool   showMaxSellInput = input.bool(true, "Max Sell row", inline="ms", group=LN_GROUP)
color  maxSellColorInput = input.color(#f23645, "", inline="ms", group=LN_GROUP)
int    lineWidthInput   = input.int(2, "Line Width", minval=1, maxval=4, group=LN_GROUP)
string lineStyleInput   = input.string(
     "Solid", "Line Style", options=["Solid", "Dashed", "Dotted"], group=LN_GROUP)
bool   extendLinesInput = input.bool(
     false, "Extend Lines Right", group=LN_GROUP,
     tooltip="Carries every marker to the right edge. Useful for the most recent " +
     "swing, cluttered when several swings are shown.")
bool   showLabelsInput  = input.bool(true, "Label The Lines", group=LN_GROUP)

// ————— Inputs: Appearance
string themeInput   = input.string(
     THEME_DARK, "Theme", options=[THEME_DARK, THEME_LIGHT], group="Appearance")
color  buyColorInput  = input.color(#089981, "Buy Color", group="Appearance")
color  sellColorInput = input.color(#f23645, "Sell Color", group="Appearance")
bool   showTableInput = input.bool(true, "Show Summary Table", group="Appearance")
string tablePosInput  = input.string(
     "Top Right", "Table Position",
     options=["Top Right", "Top Left", "Bottom Right", "Bottom Left"], group="Appearance")
bool   testModeInput  = input.bool(false, "Test Mode", group="Debug/Test")

// ————— Types
// One swing's finished profile. Holding the row arrays means a completed swing
// is scanned once and then only repositioned.
type SwingProfile
    int   leftBar
    int   rightBar
    float lo
    float step
    int   pocIdx
    int   maxBuyIdx
    int   maxSellIdx
    int   vaTopIdx
    int   vaBotIdx
    float peakRow
    float totalVol
    float buyVol
    array<float> buyRows
    array<float> sellRows
    // What share of this swing's bars were built from real intrabar data
    // rather than the bar-level model. Carried per swing because it varies:
    // TradingView's intrabar budget runs out on older history.
    int   intrabarPct
    // The price range covered SINCE this swing ended. A row outside it has not
    // been revisited, which is what "untested" means here. na while the swing
    // is still developing, because price is inside it by definition.
    float postLo
    float postHi

// One drawn level, followed until its outcome is decided. This is the only
// state in the script that exists purely to be honest about whether the levels
// work — nothing on the chart depends on it.
type TrackedLevel
    float price
    bool  isBuy
    int   bornBar
    bool  touched
    bool  resolved
    bool  won

// ————— Calculations: intrabar series
// NOTE ON PLACEMENT: this block sits above the function declarations on
// purpose. Pine resolves identifiers in textual order, so a function body
// can only see what was declared ABOVE its own declaration — and
// buildProfile() reads these arrays. Moving this below the functions is
// what produced 'Undeclared identifier "ltfVolume"' once already.
//
// request.security_lower_tf() has to live at global scope, so the arrays are
// fetched here and indexed by history offset inside buildProfile().
//
// The request itself is unconditional — Pine requires request.* calls at global
// scope and they cannot be skipped. What the guard changes is the ARGUMENT and
// whether the result is USED. A timeframe that is not lower than the chart's
// returns nothing usable, and every consumer below treats an empty result as
// "fall back to the bar model", so the two cases converge safely.
float chartSecondsForLtf = math.max(timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period), 1)
bool  intrabarUsable = useIntrabarInput and
     timeframe.in_seconds(intrabarTfInput) > 0 and
     timeframe.in_seconds(intrabarTfInput) < chartSecondsForLtf
string intrabarTf = intrabarUsable ? intrabarTfInput : timeframe.period

// ONE tuple request, not four separate ones. Four calls could in principle
// return arrays of differing length, and indexing them in lockstep would then
// read one sub-bar's price against another's volume — a corruption that would
// look like noise rather than a bug. One call cannot do that, and it costs one
// slot against the 40-request cap instead of four.
[ltfHigh, ltfLow, ltfClose, ltfVolume] = request.security_lower_tf(
     syminfo.tickerid, intrabarTf, [high, low, close, volume])

// ————— Function declarations
// @function Primary text color — the value column reads first.
getTextColor(string theme) =>
    switch theme
        THEME_LIGHT => #131722
        => #d1d4dc

// @function Secondary text color — deliberately lower contrast than the value.
getMutedColor(string theme) =>
    switch theme
        THEME_LIGHT => #4a4e58
        => #b2b5be

// @function Panel background. Low transparency on purpose: at 80+ the candles
// behind the panel win and the numbers stop being readable.
getPanelColor(string theme, int transp) =>
    color base = theme == THEME_LIGHT ? #ffffff : #131722
    color.new(base, transp)

// @function Maps a position-name string to the matching position.* constant.
tablePosFrom(string name) =>
    switch name
        "Top Left"     => position.top_left
        "Bottom Right" => position.bottom_right
        "Bottom Left"  => position.bottom_left
        => position.top_right

// @function Maps a style-name string to the matching line.style_* constant.
lineStyleFrom(string name) =>
    switch name
        "Dashed" => line.style_dashed
        "Dotted" => line.style_dotted
        => line.style_solid

// @function Compact human-readable volume ("1.24M", "345.6K").
formatVolume(float v) =>
    float av = math.abs(nz(v))
    switch
        av >= 1000000000 => str.tostring(v / 1000000000, "#.##") + "B"
        av >= 1000000    => str.tostring(v / 1000000, "#.##") + "M"
        av >= 1000       => str.tostring(v / 1000, "#.#") + "K"
        => str.tostring(nz(v), "#.#")

// @function The alert payload for a level being reached, in whichever format
// the user picked. JSON is emitted by hand rather than by str.format_json
// because the field ORDER and the exact key names are the contract a receiving
// bot is written against; a formatter that reorders them silently breaks it.
entryAlertPayload(bool isBuy, float price, float power, string format) =>
    string side = isBuy ? "buy" : "sell"
    format == "Text" ?
         "Price reached the " + str.upper(side) + " level at " +
         str.tostring(price, format.mintick) + " (strength " +
         str.tostring(power * 100, "#") + "%) on " + syminfo.ticker :
         '{"source":"swing_volume_profile"' +
         ',"symbol":"' + syminfo.ticker + '"' +
         ',"timeframe":"' + timeframe.period + '"' +
         ',"event":"level_reached"' +
         ',"side":"' + side + '"' +
         ',"price":' + str.tostring(price, format.mintick) +
         ',"strength_pct":' + str.tostring(power * 100, "#.#") +
         ',"close":' + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) +
         ',"bar_time":' + str.tostring(time) + '}'

// @function The alert payload for a reference level being reached.
levelAlertPayload(string kind, float price, string format) =>
    format == "Text" ?
         "Price reached the " + kind + " at " + str.tostring(price, format.mintick) +
         " on " + syminfo.ticker :
         '{"source":"swing_volume_profile"' +
         ',"symbol":"' + syminfo.ticker + '"' +
         ',"timeframe":"' + timeframe.period + '"' +
         ',"event":"' + kind + '"' +
         ',"price":' + str.tostring(price, format.mintick) +
         ',"close":' + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) +
         ',"bar_time":' + str.tostring(time) + '}'

// @function One label/value table row. Both colors are required arguments,
// which is what stops a cell shipping without text_color — Pine defaults cell
// text to BLACK, invisible on a dark panel.
dashRow(table t, int row, string label, string value, color labelCol, color valueCol) =>
    table.cell(t, 0, row, label, text_color=labelCol, text_size=size.small,
         text_halign=text.align_left)
    table.cell(t, 1, row, value, text_color=valueCol, text_size=size.small,
         text_halign=text.align_right)

// @function Builds the volume profile for the bars between two absolute bar
// indices. Returns na when the range is unusable, which the caller must handle.
//
// Volume is split by where each bar closed inside its own range — see the
// header. A doji contributes 50/50 rather than being dropped.
buildProfile(int leftBar, int rightBar, int rows, int scanCap) =>
    SwingProfile result = na
    int rightOff = bar_index - rightBar
    int leftOff  = math.min(bar_index - leftBar, scanCap)
    if rightOff >= 0 and leftOff > rightOff
        float hi = high[rightOff]
        float lo = low[rightOff]
        for i = rightOff to leftOff
            hi := math.max(hi, high[i])
            lo := math.min(lo, low[i])
        float span = math.max(hi - lo, syminfo.mintick)
        // Two things happen here, both of which only matter once rows go high.
        // A row finer than one tick cannot hold anything its neighbour does not,
        // so rows are reduced to the tick count the swing actually spans. And the
        // floor is one tick, NOT mintick * rows: the old floor inflated span for
        // any swing narrower than the row count, drawing a profile taller than
        // the swing it describes.
        int   useRows = math.max(5, math.min(rows, int(span / syminfo.mintick)))
        float step = span / useRows
        array<float> buys  = array.new<float>(useRows, 0.0)
        array<float> sells = array.new<float>(useRows, 0.0)
        float grandTotal = 0.0
        float grandBuy   = 0.0
        int intrabarBars = 0
        for i = rightOff to leftOff
            float barVol = nz(volume[i])
            // Intrabar path. Each sub-bar is placed at its own narrow range with
            // its own close position, so neither the spread nor the buy/sell
            // split is being modelled for this candle.
            array<float> subVol = intrabarUsable ? ltfVolume[i] : na
            array<float> subHigh  = intrabarUsable ? ltfHigh[i] : na
            array<float> subLow   = intrabarUsable ? ltfLow[i] : na
            array<float> subClose = intrabarUsable ? ltfClose[i] : na
            // The shortest array wins. They come from one request and should
            // always match, but iterating past the end of any of them would be
            // a runtime error on a user's chart, not on mine.
            int subCount = na(subVol) or na(subHigh) or na(subLow) or na(subClose) ? 0 :
                 int(math.min(math.min(array.size(subVol), array.size(subHigh)),
                 math.min(array.size(subLow), array.size(subClose))))
            if subCount > 0
                intrabarBars += 1
                for s = 0 to subCount - 1
                    float sv = nz(array.get(subVol, s))
                    if sv > 0
                        float sh = array.get(subHigh, s)
                        float sl = array.get(subLow, s)
                        float sc = array.get(subClose, s)
                        float sRange = sh - sl
                        float sBuy = sRange > 0 ? (sc - sl) / sRange : 0.5
                        sBuy := math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, sBuy))
                        int sStart = math.max(0, math.min(useRows - 1,
                             int(math.floor((sl - lo) / step))))
                        int sEnd = math.max(0, math.min(useRows - 1,
                             int(math.floor((sh - lo) / step))))
                        float sPerRow = sv / (sEnd - sStart + 1)
                        for r = sStart to sEnd
                            array.set(buys, r, array.get(buys, r) + sPerRow * sBuy)
                            array.set(sells, r, array.get(sells, r) + sPerRow * (1 - sBuy))
                        grandTotal += sv
                        grandBuy   += sv * sBuy
            else if barVol > 0
                float barRange = high[i] - low[i]
                float rangePos = barRange > 0 ? (close[i] - low[i]) / barRange : 0.5
                float buyShare = rangePos
                if splitModeInput == SPLIT_BLEND
                    // The tick rule, at bar resolution: which way the bar closed
                    // relative to the last one. Averaged with the range position
                    // rather than replacing it, because each is wrong in a
                    // different place and neither is order flow.
                    float dirShare = close[i] > close[i + 1] ? 1.0 :
                         close[i] < close[i + 1] ? 0.0 : 0.5
                    buyShare := (rangePos + dirShare) / 2
                buyShare := math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, buyShare))
                int startIdx = int(math.floor((low[i] - lo) / step))
                int endIdx   = int(math.floor((high[i] - lo) / step))
                startIdx := math.max(0, math.min(useRows - 1, startIdx))
                endIdx   := math.max(0, math.min(useRows - 1, endIdx))
                // Body rows carry the bar's volume; wick rows carry a fraction of
                // it. A wick is a price the market REJECTED and traded through
                // quickly, so giving it the same volume per row as the body
                // overstates every extreme in the profile — which is precisely
                // where the levels people trade get drawn.
                int bodyStart = startIdx
                int bodyEnd = endIdx
                float wickWeight = 1.0
                if distModeInput == DIST_BODY
                    float bodyLo = math.min(open[i], close[i])
                    float bodyHi = math.max(open[i], close[i])
                    bodyStart := math.max(startIdx, math.min(useRows - 1,
                         int(math.floor((bodyLo - lo) / step))))
                    bodyEnd := math.min(endIdx, math.max(0,
                         int(math.floor((bodyHi - lo) / step))))
                    if bodyEnd < bodyStart
                        bodyEnd := bodyStart
                    wickWeight := WICK_WEIGHT
                int bodyCount = bodyEnd - bodyStart + 1
                int wickCount = (endIdx - startIdx + 1) - bodyCount
                // Closed form, so the weighting costs no extra pass over the rows.
                float weightSum = bodyCount + wickCount * wickWeight
                float unitVol = barVol / math.max(weightSum, 1e-10)
                for r = startIdx to endIdx
                    float w = r >= bodyStart and r <= bodyEnd ? 1.0 : wickWeight
                    float rowVol = unitVol * w
                    array.set(buys, r, array.get(buys, r) + rowVol * buyShare)
                    array.set(sells, r, array.get(sells, r) + rowVol * (1 - buyShare))
                grandTotal += barVol
                grandBuy   += barVol * buyShare
        // Recorded so the table can say how much of the profile is measured and
        // how much is modelled. A number whose provenance is invisible invites
        // more trust than it has earned.
        int scanned = leftOff - rightOff + 1
        int intrabarPct = scanned > 0 ? int(intrabarBars * 100 / scanned) : 0
        // Three separate peaks: the row that traded most in total, the row that
        // absorbed the most buying, and the row that absorbed the most selling.
        // They are often different rows, and that difference is the signal.
        float peakTotal = 0.0
        float peakBuy   = 0.0
        float peakSell  = 0.0
        int   pocIdx = 0
        int   buyIdx = 0
        int   sellIdx = 0
        for r = 0 to useRows - 1
            float b = array.get(buys, r)
            float s = array.get(sells, r)
            if b + s > peakTotal
                peakTotal := b + s
                pocIdx := r
            if b > peakBuy
                peakBuy := b
                buyIdx := r
            if s > peakSell
                peakSell := s
                sellIdx := r
        // Value area: grow outward from the POC, always taking the heavier
        // neighbour, until the target share of the swing's volume is inside.
        // This is the standard construction, and standard matters here — other
        // traders are drawing the same two lines.
        int   vaTop = pocIdx
        int   vaBot = pocIdx
        float vaTarget = grandTotal * valueAreaPctInput / 100
        float vaVol = array.get(buys, pocIdx) + array.get(sells, pocIdx)
        // Bounded by the row count: each pass moves at least one edge, so it can
        // never run longer than there are rows, whatever the volume looks like.
        for _step = 1 to useRows
            if vaVol >= vaTarget or (vaBot <= 0 and vaTop >= useRows - 1)
                break
            float above = vaTop < useRows - 1 ?
                 array.get(buys, vaTop + 1) + array.get(sells, vaTop + 1) : -1.0
            float below = vaBot > 0 ?
                 array.get(buys, vaBot - 1) + array.get(sells, vaBot - 1) : -1.0
            if above >= below
                vaTop += 1
                vaVol += above
            else
                vaBot -= 1
                vaVol += below
        if peakTotal > 0
            result := SwingProfile.new(
                 leftBar = math.max(leftBar, bar_index - leftOff), rightBar = rightBar,
                 lo = lo, step = step, pocIdx = pocIdx, maxBuyIdx = buyIdx,
                 maxSellIdx = sellIdx, vaTopIdx = vaTop, vaBotIdx = vaBot,
                 peakRow = peakTotal, totalVol = grandTotal,
                 buyVol = grandBuy, buyRows = buys, sellRows = sells,
                 postLo = na, postHi = na, intrabarPct = intrabarPct)
    result

// @function Price at the centre of a profile row.
rowPrice(SwingProfile p, int idx) =>
    p.lo + (idx + 0.5) * p.step

// @function The strongest volume imbalance on each side of a price.
//
// Returns [buyIdx, buyStrength, sellIdx, sellStrength]; an index of -1 means
// that side has nothing worth drawing.
//
// The score is (buy - sell) / peakRow, which is one number carrying both things
// that matter: how lopsided the row was, scaled by how much volume it held.
// A row where buyers barely won on tiny volume scores near zero, and so does a
// huge row that was evenly matched. Neither is a level. What scores high is a
// price where one side traded heavily and the other side did not answer.
//
// Priced off the row CENTRES, and evaluated against live price, so this is the
// only part of the drawing that has to run on every tick.
bestEntry(SwingProfile p, float price, float minStrength) =>
    int rows = array.size(p.buyRows)
    int   buyIdx = -1
    int   sellIdx = -1
    float buyBest = minStrength
    float sellBest = minStrength
    // A developing swing has no "since" to test against, so it is never
    // filtered — na(postLo) is the signal for that, not a missing value.
    bool canFilter = preferUntestedInput and not na(p.postLo) and not na(p.postHi)
    float volFloor = p.peakRow * entryMinVolumeInput / 100
    if p.peakRow > 0 and rows > 0
        for r = 0 to rows - 1
            float rowMid = p.lo + (r + 0.5) * p.step
            bool untested = not canFilter or rowMid > p.postHi or rowMid < p.postLo
            float rowBuy = array.get(p.buyRows, r)
            float rowSell = array.get(p.sellRows, r)
            // Two independent floors. Strength says how one-sided the row was;
            // this says whether enough traded there for one-sidedness to mean
            // anything. A thin shelf can be 90% buying and still be nothing —
            // price does not defend a price nobody was at.
            bool bigEnough = rowBuy + rowSell >= volFloor
            float imbalance = (rowBuy - rowSell) / p.peakRow
            if untested and bigEnough and rowMid < price and imbalance > buyBest
                buyBest := imbalance
                buyIdx := r
            if untested and bigEnough and rowMid > price and -imbalance > sellBest
                sellBest := -imbalance
                sellIdx := r
    [buyIdx, buyIdx >= 0 ? buyBest : 0.0, sellIdx, sellIdx >= 0 ? sellBest : 0.0]

// @function Grows a box pool, parking new boxes invisibly.
ensureBoxPool(array<box> pool, int needed) =>
    while array.size(pool) < needed
        array.push(pool, box.new(
             bar_index, close, bar_index, close, xloc=xloc.bar_index,
             bgcolor=TRANSPARENT_COLOR, border_color=TRANSPARENT_COLOR))

// @function Grows a line pool.
ensureLinePool(array<line> pool, int needed) =>
    while array.size(pool) < needed
        array.push(pool, line.new(
             bar_index, close, bar_index, close, xloc=xloc.bar_index,
             color=TRANSPARENT_COLOR))

// @function Grows a label pool.
ensureLabelPool(array<label> pool, int needed) =>
    while array.size(pool) < needed
        array.push(pool, label.new(
             bar_index, close, "", xloc=xloc.bar_index,
             style=label.style_label_left, color=TRANSPARENT_COLOR))

// @function Hides pool boxes from `from` onward so a shrinking draw leaves
// nothing stale behind.
hideBoxesFrom(array<box> pool, int from) =>
    if array.size(pool) > from
        for i = from to array.size(pool) - 1
            box.set_bgcolor(array.get(pool, i), TRANSPARENT_COLOR)
            box.set_border_color(array.get(pool, i), TRANSPARENT_COLOR)

// @function Hides pool lines from `from` onward.
hideLinesFrom(array<line> pool, int from) =>
    if array.size(pool) > from
        for i = from to array.size(pool) - 1
            line.set_color(array.get(pool, i), TRANSPARENT_COLOR)

// @function Hides pool labels from `from` onward.
hideLabelsFrom(array<label> pool, int from) =>
    if array.size(pool) > from
        for i = from to array.size(pool) - 1
            label.set_text(array.get(pool, i), "")

// ————— Calculations: budget
// Boxes are capped at 500 for the whole script, and the profile rows are the
// only consumer — the marker lines and their tags come out of the separate line
// and label pools.
//
// Rows are BUILT at the resolution you ask for, always. That is the part that
// decides how precise POC / Max Buy / Max Sell are, and it costs arrays, not
// boxes. Only the drawing is rationed, and it is rationed by spending boxes on
// rows that actually hold volume — at high resolution most rows in a swing are
// empty, and an empty row now costs nothing at all. Newest swing draws first, so
// if the budget does run out it is the oldest swing that thins, not the live one.
int boxesPerRow    = showSplitInput ? 2 : 1
int profileRows    = rowsInput

color textColor  = getTextColor(themeInput)
color mutedColor = getMutedColor(themeInput)
string markerStyle = lineStyleFrom(lineStyleInput)

// ————— Calculations: bar delta
//
// The same split the profile uses, applied to ONE bar. Intrabar when it is
// available — each sub-bar contributes its own close position — and the
// bar-level model otherwise, exactly as in buildProfile(). Keeping the two in
// step matters: a bubble that disagreed with the profile beneath it would be
// two different claims about the same volume.
float barBuyVol  = 0.0
float barSellVol = 0.0
int   barSubCount = intrabarUsable and not na(ltfVolume) and not na(ltfHigh) and
     not na(ltfLow) and not na(ltfClose) ?
     int(math.min(math.min(array.size(ltfVolume), array.size(ltfHigh)),
     math.min(array.size(ltfLow), array.size(ltfClose)))) : 0
if barSubCount > 0
    for s = 0 to barSubCount - 1
        float sv = nz(array.get(ltfVolume, s))
        if sv > 0
            float sh = array.get(ltfHigh, s)
            float sl = array.get(ltfLow, s)
            float sc = array.get(ltfClose, s)
            float sRange = sh - sl
            float sBuy = sRange > 0 ? (sc - sl) / sRange : 0.5
            sBuy := math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, sBuy))
            barBuyVol  += sv * sBuy
            barSellVol += sv * (1 - sBuy)
else
    float rangeNow = high - low
    float buyNow = rangeNow > 0 ? (close - low) / rangeNow : 0.5
    buyNow := math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, buyNow))
    barBuyVol  := nz(volume) * buyNow
    barSellVol := nz(volume) * (1 - buyNow)

float barVolTotal = barBuyVol + barSellVol
float barDeltaPct = barVolTotal > 0 ? (barBuyVol - barSellVol) / barVolTotal : 0.0
float bubbleVolAvg = ta.sma(volume, bubbleVolLenInput)
float bubbleVolRatio = nz(bubbleVolAvg) > 0 ? nz(volume) / bubbleVolAvg : 0.0
float bubbleFloor = bubbleDeltaInput / 100

// Both floors, plus agreement with the candle itself. A bar with heavy BUY
// delta that still closed down is not weak buying — it is buying being
// absorbed, which is a different thing this script already reports separately.
// Marking it green here would contradict that.
bool strongBull = showBubblesInput and close > open and
     barDeltaPct >= bubbleFloor and bubbleVolRatio >= bubbleVolInput
bool strongBear = showBubblesInput and close < open and
     -barDeltaPct >= bubbleFloor and bubbleVolRatio >= bubbleVolInput

// Shade carries the surplus above the threshold, so a bar that only just
// qualifies reads faintly and an overwhelming one reads solid. Free: the colour
// argument of plotshape takes a series, so this costs no extra plot slots.
float bullSurplus = math.min(1.0, (barDeltaPct - bubbleFloor) /
     math.max(1.0 - bubbleFloor, 0.0001))
float bearSurplus = math.min(1.0, (-barDeltaPct - bubbleFloor) /
     math.max(1.0 - bubbleFloor, 0.0001))
color bullBubbleColor = color.new(bubbleBullColorInput, int(55 - 45 * nz(bullSurplus)))
color bearBubbleColor = color.new(bubbleBearColorInput, int(55 - 45 * nz(bearSurplus)))

// ————— Calculations: segment detection
//
// Three sources, one output: a boolean saying a leg just ended, and the bar
// index where it ended. Everything downstream — the profile, the value area,
// the levels, the drawing — only ever needed those two things, so all three
// sources reuse the whole pipeline rather than forking it.

// Source 1: pivots on the chart timeframe.
float pivotHigh = ta.pivothigh(high, pivotLenInput, pivotLenInput)
float pivotLow  = ta.pivotlow(low, pivotLenInput, pivotLenInput)
bool  chartPivot = not na(pivotHigh) or not na(pivotLow)

// Source 2: pivots on a higher timeframe. The price is exact; the BAR is
// converted by the ratio of the two timeframes, which is exact within a session
// and drifts by a bar or two across gaps. Said plainly in the input tooltip
// rather than buried here, because a user choosing this option deserves to know
// before they choose it.
bool useHtf = swingSourceInput == SRC_HTF
[htfPivotHigh, htfPivotLow] = request.security(
     syminfo.tickerid, useHtf ? htfInput : timeframe.period,
     [ta.pivothigh(high, pivotLenInput, pivotLenInput),
      ta.pivotlow(low, pivotLenInput, pivotLenInput)],
     lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off)
// "A new HTF pivot" means the value CHANGED, not merely that one exists: the
// security call republishes the same pivot on every chart bar until the next
// one forms. Each side is tested separately so an na on one cannot make the
// other look new.
bool htfNewHigh = not na(htfPivotHigh) and (na(htfPivotHigh[1]) or htfPivotHigh != htfPivotHigh[1])
bool htfNewLow  = not na(htfPivotLow) and (na(htfPivotLow[1]) or htfPivotLow != htfPivotLow[1])
bool htfPivot   = useHtf and (htfNewHigh or htfNewLow)
float htfSeconds = timeframe.in_seconds(htfInput)
float chartSeconds = math.max(timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period), 1)
int htfBarRatio = math.max(1, int(htfSeconds / chartSeconds))

// Source 3: sessions. Exact, because a session boundary is a clock time and
// needs no conversion at all.
bool inSession = not na(time(timeframe.period, sessionInput))
bool wasInSession = inSession[1]
bool sessionEnded = swingSourceInput == SRC_SESSION and not inSession and wasInSession

bool newSegment = switch swingSourceInput
    SRC_HTF     => htfPivot
    SRC_SESSION => sessionEnded
    => chartPivot
int segmentBar = switch swingSourceInput
    SRC_HTF     => bar_index - pivotLenInput * htfBarRatio
    SRC_SESSION => bar_index - 1
    => bar_index - pivotLenInput
// A converted or session-derived bar must still be a real, in-range bar.
segmentBar := math.max(0, math.min(bar_index, segmentBar))


// Absolute bar index of every confirmed pivot, newest last.
//
// Recorded ON BAR CLOSE, and that is not a detail. ta.pivothigh's window
// includes the bar still forming, so a pivot can confirm on one tick and be
// invalidated by a higher high on the next. `var` does NOT protect against
// that: it restores the variable on a realtime rollback, never the contents of
// the array the variable points at, so a push that happened on a tick is
// permanent whether or not the pivot survived. Earlier versions of this file
// claimed otherwise in a comment right here, and were wrong.
var array<int> pivotBars = array.new<int>()
var array<SwingProfile> swings = array.new<SwingProfile>()

// Bumped by every change to the set of profiles. The drawing compares against
// it rather than against the swing count, because a push plus a drop on the
// same tick leaves the count identical and the picture wrong.
var int profileGen = 0

// Level tracking. Nothing on the chart depends on this; it exists so the
// indicator can be honest about whether its own levels hold.
var array<TrackedLevel> tracked = array.new<TrackedLevel>()
var int statWins = 0
var int statResolved = 0
float atrValue = ta.atr(STAT_ATR_LEN)

if newSegment and barstate.isconfirmed
    int pivotBar = segmentBar
    // Build the profile for the leg that just closed, then keep only the most
    // recent ones. A completed swing never changes, so this runs once per swing.
    if array.size(pivotBars) > 0
        int prevBar = array.get(pivotBars, array.size(pivotBars) - 1)
        SwingProfile finished = buildProfile(prevBar, pivotBar, profileRows, maxSwingBarsInput)
        if not na(finished)
            array.push(swings, finished)
            profileGen += 1
            // Record what this swing WOULD have offered, at the moment it
            // closed, so the hit rate measures the same levels the chart draws
            // rather than some easier-to-compute proxy.
            if showStatsInput
                [statBuyIdx, _statBuyPow, statSellIdx, _statSellPow] =
                     bestEntry(finished, close, entryMinStrengthInput / 100)
                if statBuyIdx >= 0
                    array.push(tracked, TrackedLevel.new(
                         price = rowPrice(finished, statBuyIdx), isBuy = true,
                         bornBar = bar_index, touched = false, resolved = false, won = false))
                if statSellIdx >= 0
                    array.push(tracked, TrackedLevel.new(
                         price = rowPrice(finished, statSellIdx), isBuy = false,
                         bornBar = bar_index, touched = false, resolved = false, won = false))
                while array.size(tracked) > statMaxLevelsInput
                    array.shift(tracked)
            while array.size(swings) > maxSwingsInput
                array.shift(swings)
                profileGen += 1
    array.push(pivotBars, pivotBar)
    while array.size(pivotBars) > maxSwingsInput + 2
        array.shift(pivotBars)

// The developing leg: last confirmed pivot to the current bar. Memoised on
// bar_index so it recomputes once per bar rather than on every realtime tick —
// its shape cannot meaningfully change within one 5-minute candle.
var SwingProfile liveSwing = na
var int liveCachedBar = na
if showLiveSwingInput and array.size(pivotBars) > 0
    bool needsRebuild = na(liveCachedBar) or liveCachedBar != bar_index
    if needsRebuild
        liveCachedBar := bar_index
        int lastPivot = array.get(pivotBars, array.size(pivotBars) - 1)
        liveSwing := buildProfile(lastPivot, bar_index, profileRows, maxSwingBarsInput)
        profileGen += 1

// ————— Calculations: level tracking
//
// Two passes per CLOSED bar, both over a handful of items. The first keeps each
// stored swing's "range covered since it ended" current, which is what makes an
// untested row cheap to identify — no rescanning history, one min and one max.
if barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(swings) > 0
    for i = 0 to array.size(swings) - 1
        SwingProfile sp = array.get(swings, i)
        if sp.rightBar < bar_index
            sp.postLo := na(sp.postLo) ? low : math.min(sp.postLo, low)
            sp.postHi := na(sp.postHi) ? high : math.max(sp.postHi, high)

// The second decides outcomes. A level is touched when price trades through it,
// and resolved when price has since travelled the target distance one way or
// the other.
//
// The stop is checked BEFORE the target on purpose. When a single bar covers
// both, the order they happened in is unknowable from bar data, and calling
// that a win would inflate every number in this block. It is counted as a loss.
if showStatsInput and barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(tracked) > 0
    float statTarget = nz(atrValue) * statTargetAtrInput
    if statTarget > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(tracked) - 1
            TrackedLevel lv = array.get(tracked, i)
            if not lv.resolved and bar_index > lv.bornBar
                if not lv.touched and low <= lv.price and high >= lv.price
                    lv.touched := true
                if lv.touched
                    float favourable = lv.isBuy ? high - lv.price : lv.price - low
                    float adverse    = lv.isBuy ? lv.price - low : high - lv.price
                    if adverse >= statTarget
                        lv.resolved := true
                        statResolved += 1
                    else if favourable >= statTarget
                        lv.resolved := true
                        lv.won := true
                        statWins += 1
                        statResolved += 1

// ————— Calculations: absorption
//
// A new extreme made on RISING opposite-side participation. The extreme says
// one side pushed; the rising share says the other side was there to take it.
// Both halves are needed — a new low on rising selling is just a downtrend.
var string absorptionState = "none"
var int    absorptionBar = na
if showAbsorptionInput and array.size(swings) >= 2
    SwingProfile newer = array.get(swings, array.size(swings) - 1)
    SwingProfile older = array.get(swings, array.size(swings) - 2)
    if newer.totalVol > 0 and older.totalVol > 0
        float newerBuyShare = newer.buyVol / newer.totalVol
        float olderBuyShare = older.buyVol / older.totalVol
        float newerTop = newer.lo + array.size(newer.buyRows) * newer.step
        float olderTop = older.lo + array.size(older.buyRows) * older.step
        string found = "none"
        if newer.lo < older.lo and newerBuyShare > olderBuyShare
            found := "bullish"
        else if newerTop > olderTop and newerBuyShare < olderBuyShare
            found := "bearish"
        if found != "none" and (na(absorptionBar) or absorptionBar != newer.rightBar)
            absorptionState := found
            absorptionBar := newer.rightBar
        else if found == "none"
            absorptionState := "none"

// ————— Calculations: naked POC and value migration
//
// A naked POC is the most-agreed price of a finished leg that price has NOT
// come back to. The postLo/postHi range each swing already maintains makes this
// a comparison rather than a search.
//
// Value migration is where the POC went from one finished swing to the next.
// It is the cheapest read of control there is: value moving up means each leg
// is finding agreement higher than the last.
float nakedPocPrice = na
string valueMigration = "--"
if array.size(swings) > 0
    float nearest = na
    for i = 0 to array.size(swings) - 1
        SwingProfile sp = array.get(swings, i)
        float pocP = rowPrice(sp, sp.pocIdx)
        // na postLo means the swing has no "since" yet, so nothing can be naked.
        if not na(sp.postLo) and not na(sp.postHi) and (pocP > sp.postHi or pocP < sp.postLo)
            if na(nearest) or math.abs(pocP - close) < nearest
                nearest := math.abs(pocP - close)
                nakedPocPrice := pocP
    if array.size(swings) >= 2
        SwingProfile newest = array.get(swings, array.size(swings) - 1)
        SwingProfile prior  = array.get(swings, array.size(swings) - 2)
        float pocNew = rowPrice(newest, newest.pocIdx)
        float pocOld = rowPrice(prior, prior.pocIdx)
        // A tick of movement is not migration; it is rounding.
        float migrationFloor = math.max(syminfo.mintick, newest.step)
        valueMigration := pocNew > pocOld + migrationFloor ? "UP" :
             pocNew < pocOld - migrationFloor ? "DOWN" : "FLAT"

// ————— Visuals: pools
var array<box>   rowBoxes    = array.new<box>()
var int          boxesUsed   = 0
var array<line>  markerLines = array.new<line>()
var array<label> markerTags  = array.new<label>()

// The entry level is the one drawing that depends on live price, so it lives in
// its own pool and updates on every tick while everything else does not.
var array<line>  entryLines = array.new<line>()
var array<label> entryTags  = array.new<label>()
var array<label> volumeTags = array.new<label>()
var array<box>   zoneBoxes  = array.new<box>()
var array<label> zoneTags   = array.new<label>()
var int          zoneCount  = 0

// Every reference price currently drawn, refilled each redraw and then clustered.
var array<float> levelPool = array.new<float>()

// Live state, published so the table and the alerts read the same numbers the
// chart is showing rather than recomputing them and drifting.
var float entryBuyPrice   = na
var float entrySellPrice  = na
var float entryBuyPower   = 0.0
var float entrySellPower  = 0.0


// @function Draws one swing's rows into the box pool starting at `cursor`,
// stopping before box index `limit`, and returns how many boxes it used.
//
// A row holding no volume consumes NO box. That is what makes a high row count
// affordable: the rows a profile actually needs are the ones price traded in.
drawRows(SwingProfile p, array<box> pool, int cursor, int limit, int rightEdge, int leftEdge) =>
    int used = 0
    int rows = array.size(p.buyRows)
    int cost = showSplitInput ? 2 : 1
    int fullWidth = math.max(1, rightEdge - leftEdge)
    for r = 0 to rows - 1
        // Out of budget: every remaining row would be skipped anyway, and at 500
        // rows a swing the wasted iterations are not free.
        if cursor + used + cost > limit
            break
        float b = array.get(p.buyRows, r)
        float s = array.get(p.sellRows, r)
        float t = b + s
        float yBot = p.lo + r * p.step
        if t > 0 and p.peakRow > 0
            int rowWidth = math.max(1, int(fullWidth * (t / p.peakRow)))
            if showSplitInput
                // Sell segment first, then buy — so the split reads left to
                // right in the same order every time.
                int sellWidth = math.max(0, int(rowWidth * (t > 0 ? s / t : 0.0)))
                box sellBox = array.get(pool, cursor + used)
                box.set_lefttop(sellBox, leftEdge, yBot + p.step)
                box.set_rightbottom(sellBox, leftEdge + sellWidth, yBot)
                box.set_bgcolor(sellBox, color.new(sellColorInput, rowTranspInput))
                box.set_border_color(sellBox, TRANSPARENT_COLOR)
                used += 1
                box buyBox = array.get(pool, cursor + used)
                box.set_lefttop(buyBox, leftEdge + sellWidth, yBot + p.step)
                box.set_rightbottom(buyBox, leftEdge + rowWidth, yBot)
                box.set_bgcolor(buyBox, color.new(buyColorInput, rowTranspInput))
                box.set_border_color(buyBox, TRANSPARENT_COLOR)
                used += 1
            else
                box bx = array.get(pool, cursor + used)
                box.set_lefttop(bx, leftEdge, yBot + p.step)
                box.set_rightbottom(bx, leftEdge + rowWidth, yBot)
                // A low volume node is worth seeing precisely because it is
                // almost invisible at this width — price runs through it.
                bool isLvn = showLvnInput and t < p.peakRow * lvnPctInput / 100
                box.set_bgcolor(bx, color.new(
                     isLvn ? lvnColorInput : b >= s ? buyColorInput : sellColorInput,
                     rowTranspInput))
                box.set_border_color(bx, TRANSPARENT_COLOR)
                used += 1
    used

// @function Groups nearby prices into zones and draws a band for each group
// that has at least `minMembers` levels in it. Returns how many zones it drew.
//
// The prices arrive unsorted, so this is an insertion sort followed by a single
// sweep — n is the number of reference lines on the chart, a few dozen at most,
// and an O(n^2) sort on a few dozen items once per bar is free.
drawConfluence(array<float> prices, float tolerance, int minMembers,
     array<box> boxes, array<label> tags, int leftX, int rightX) =>
    int drawn = 0
    int n = array.size(prices)
    if n > 0 and tolerance > 0
        array.sort(prices, order.ascending)
        int i = 0
        while i < n and drawn < array.size(boxes)
            int j = i
            // Extend the cluster while the next price is within tolerance of
            // the one before it, not of the first — otherwise a long ladder of
            // close levels would never close.
            while j + 1 < n and array.get(prices, j + 1) - array.get(prices, j) <= tolerance
                j += 1
            int members = j - i + 1
            if members >= minMembers
                float lo = array.get(prices, i)
                float hi = array.get(prices, j)
                box bx = array.get(boxes, drawn)
                box.set_lefttop(bx, leftX, hi)
                box.set_rightbottom(bx, rightX, lo)
                box.set_bgcolor(bx, color.new(confluenceColorInput, 82))
                box.set_border_color(bx, color.new(confluenceColorInput, 40))
                label lb = array.get(tags, drawn)
                label.set_xy(lb, rightX, (hi + lo) / 2)
                label.set_text(lb, str.tostring(members) + "x")
                label.set_textcolor(lb, confluenceColorInput)
                label.set_size(lb, size.small)
                label.set_style(lb, label.style_label_left)
                label.set_color(lb, TRANSPARENT_COLOR)
                label.set_tooltip(lb, str.tostring(members) + " reference levels agree here" +
                     "\nZone: " + str.tostring(lo, format.mintick) + " - " +
                     str.tostring(hi, format.mintick))
                drawn += 1
            i := j + 1
    drawn

// @function Prints a swing's total traded volume above its profile.
// `settled` is false for the leg still forming, whose number is still moving.
drawVolumeTag(SwingProfile p, label lb, int x, float topPrice, bool settled) =>
    float buyPct = p.totalVol > 0 ? p.buyVol / p.totalVol * 100 : na
    string prefix = settled ? "" : "~"
    label.set_xy(lb, x, topPrice)
    label.set_text(lb, prefix + formatVolume(p.totalVol) +
         (na(buyPct) ? "" : "  " + str.tostring(buyPct, "#") + "% buy"))
    label.set_textcolor(lb, na(buyPct) or buyPct >= 50 ? buyColorInput : sellColorInput)
    label.set_size(lb, size.small)
    label.set_style(lb, label.style_label_down)
    label.set_color(lb, TRANSPARENT_COLOR)
    label.set_tooltip(lb, (settled ? "Completed swing" : "Developing swing — still changing") +
         "\nTotal volume: " + formatVolume(p.totalVol) +
         "\nBuying: " + formatVolume(p.buyVol) +
         "\nSelling: " + formatVolume(p.totalVol - p.buyVol) +
         "\nBars: " + str.tostring(p.rightBar - p.leftBar + 1) +
         "\nRows: " + str.tostring(array.size(p.buyRows)))

// @function Draws one marker line (and optional label), returning 1 if it was
// used so the caller can advance its cursor.
drawMarker(bool enabled, float price, string tag, color col, array<line> linePool,
     array<label> labelPool, int lineCursor, int labelCursor, int leftEdge, int rightEdge) =>
    int used = 0
    if enabled and not na(price)
        line ln = array.get(linePool, lineCursor)
        line.set_xy1(ln, leftEdge, price)
        line.set_xy2(ln, rightEdge, price)
        line.set_color(ln, col)
        line.set_width(ln, lineWidthInput)
        line.set_style(ln, markerStyle)
        line.set_extend(ln, extendLinesInput ? extend.right : extend.none)
        label lb = array.get(labelPool, labelCursor)
        if showLabelsInput
            label.set_xy(lb, rightEdge, price)
            label.set_text(lb, tag + "  " + str.tostring(price, format.mintick))
            label.set_textcolor(lb, col)
            label.set_size(lb, size.small)
        else
            label.set_text(lb, "")
        used := 1
    used

// @function Draws one entry level and its label.
drawEntry(line ln, label lb, float price, float power, bool isBuy, int leftBar) =>
    color col = isBuy ? buyColorInput : sellColorInput
    line.set_xy1(ln, leftBar, price)
    line.set_xy2(ln, bar_index, price)
    line.set_color(ln, col)
    line.set_width(ln, entryWidthInput)
    line.set_style(ln, line.style_solid)
    line.set_extend(ln, entryExtendInput ? extend.right : extend.none)
    label.set_xy(lb, bar_index, price)
    label.set_text(lb, (isBuy ? "BUY " : "SELL ") + str.tostring(price, format.mintick) +
         "  " + str.tostring(power * 100, "#") + "%")
    label.set_textcolor(lb, col)
    label.set_size(lb, size.small)
    label.set_style(lb, label.style_label_left)
    label.set_color(lb, TRANSPARENT_COLOR)
    label.set_tooltip(lb,
         (isBuy ? "Strongest BUY imbalance below price" : "Strongest SELL imbalance above price") +
         "\nStrength: " + str.tostring(power * 100, "#.#") + "% of the busiest row" +
         "\nDistance: " + str.tostring(math.abs(close - price), format.mintick) +
         "\n\nThis is a level, not a signal. It marks where one side traded " +
         "heavily and the other did not answer — it says nothing about trend, " +
         "direction or timing.")


// ————— Visuals: the profile, redrawn once per bar
//
// Nothing in this block depends on live price, and the profiles themselves only
// change when a bar closes or a pivot confirms. Running it on every tick meant
// up to 492 box .set_*() calls to redraw a picture that was already correct —
// the single most expensive thing this script did, repeated for no reason. The
// entry level, which genuinely does track price, is drawn separately below.
//
// The generation counter is part of the trigger, not just the bar: a pivot can
// confirm mid-bar, and a new swing must not wait for the next candle to appear.
var int drawnBar = na
var int drawnGen = -1
int swingCount = array.size(swings)
int liveCount  = na(liveSwing) ? 0 : 1
int totalSwings = swingCount + liveCount
bool profileNeedsRedraw = na(drawnBar) or drawnBar != bar_index or drawnGen != profileGen

if barstate.islast and profileNeedsRedraw
    drawnBar := bar_index
    drawnGen := profileGen
    // Ask for what the swings would use, capped by what TradingView allows.
    int poolSize = math.min(
         MAX_ROW_BOXES, math.max(1, totalSwings * profileRows * boxesPerRow))
    ensureBoxPool(rowBoxes, poolSize)
    ensureLinePool(markerLines, math.max(1, totalSwings * MARKERS_PER_SWING))
    ensureLabelPool(markerTags, math.max(1, totalSwings * MARKERS_PER_SWING))
    ensureLabelPool(volumeTags, math.max(1, totalSwings))

    int boxCursor = 0
    int lineCursor = 0
    int tagCursor = 0
    array.clear(levelPool)
    // The guard is not cosmetic: Pine counts DOWN when a for-loop's end value is
    // below its start, so `0 to totalSwings - 1` with no swings yet would run
    // with i = 0 and i = -1 and index an empty array.
    if totalSwings > 0
        for k = 0 to totalSwings - 1
            // Newest first. If the box budget runs out it must be the oldest
            // swing that loses rows, never the leg price is trading in now.
            int i = totalSwings - 1 - k
            SwingProfile p = i < swingCount ? array.get(swings, i) : liveSwing
            if not na(p)
                int leftEdge = p.leftBar
                int rightEdge = p.rightBar
                if profilePosInput == "Right Of Swing"
                    leftEdge := p.rightBar
                    rightEdge := p.rightBar + profileWidthInput
                else
                    // Cap how far the longest row reaches so the profile never
                    // buries the candles it is describing.
                    int swingWidth = math.max(1, p.rightBar - p.leftBar)
                    rightEdge := p.leftBar + math.max(1, int(swingWidth * maxWidthPctInput / 100))
                boxCursor += drawRows(p, rowBoxes, boxCursor, poolSize, rightEdge, leftEdge)
                int markerRight = profilePosInput == "Right Of Swing" ?
                     p.rightBar + profileWidthInput : p.rightBar
                lineCursor += drawMarker(
                     showPocInput, rowPrice(p, p.pocIdx), "POC", pocColorInput,
                     markerLines, markerTags, lineCursor, lineCursor, p.leftBar, markerRight)
                lineCursor += drawMarker(
                     showMaxBuyInput, rowPrice(p, p.maxBuyIdx), "MAX BUY", maxBuyColorInput,
                     markerLines, markerTags, lineCursor, lineCursor, p.leftBar, markerRight)
                lineCursor += drawMarker(
                     showMaxSellInput, rowPrice(p, p.maxSellIdx), "MAX SELL", maxSellColorInput,
                     markerLines, markerTags, lineCursor, lineCursor, p.leftBar, markerRight)
                // The value area's EDGES are what people trade — price leaving
                // the area is the market disagreeing with the price it spent
                // most of the swing agreeing on.
                lineCursor += drawMarker(
                     showValueAreaInput, rowPrice(p, p.vaTopIdx), "VAH", vaColorInput,
                     markerLines, markerTags, lineCursor, lineCursor, p.leftBar, markerRight)
                lineCursor += drawMarker(
                     showValueAreaInput, rowPrice(p, p.vaBotIdx), "VAL", vaColorInput,
                     markerLines, markerTags, lineCursor, lineCursor, p.leftBar, markerRight)
                // Collected for the confluence pass. Only levels that are
                // actually DRAWN go in — a zone built from hidden lines would
                // point at agreement the user cannot see.
                if showConfluenceInput
                    if showPocInput
                        array.push(levelPool, rowPrice(p, p.pocIdx))
                    if showMaxBuyInput
                        array.push(levelPool, rowPrice(p, p.maxBuyIdx))
                    if showMaxSellInput
                        array.push(levelPool, rowPrice(p, p.maxSellIdx))
                    if showValueAreaInput
                        array.push(levelPool, rowPrice(p, p.vaTopIdx))
                        array.push(levelPool, rowPrice(p, p.vaBotIdx))
                if showVolumeTagInput
                    float profileTop = p.lo + array.size(p.buyRows) * p.step
                    drawVolumeTag(p, array.get(volumeTags, tagCursor),
                         int((leftEdge + rightEdge) / 2), profileTop, i < swingCount)
                    tagCursor += 1
    boxesUsed := boxCursor
    hideBoxesFrom(rowBoxes, boxCursor)
    hideLinesFrom(markerLines, lineCursor)
    hideLabelsFrom(markerTags, lineCursor)
    hideLabelsFrom(volumeTags, tagCursor)

    // Confluence, drawn last because it needs every level the pass collected.
    // Its boxes come from the 8 held back from MAX_ROW_BOXES, so a busy profile
    // can never starve it.
    ensureBoxPool(zoneBoxes, 8)
    ensureLabelPool(zoneTags, 8)
    zoneCount := 0
    if showConfluenceInput and totalSwings > 0
        int zoneRight = bar_index
        int zoneLeft = bar_index - math.max(10, maxSwingBarsInput / 4)
        zoneCount := drawConfluence(
             levelPool, nz(atrValue) * confluenceAtrInput, confluenceMinInput,
             zoneBoxes, zoneTags, zoneLeft, zoneRight)
    hideBoxesFrom(zoneBoxes, zoneCount)
    hideLabelsFrom(zoneTags, zoneCount)

// ————— Visuals: the entry level, every tick
//
// This is the only drawing that depends on live price, so it is the only one
// that has to be recomputed while a bar is open. One pass over the rows.
if barstate.islast
    ensureLinePool(entryLines, 2)
    ensureLabelPool(entryTags, 2)
    entryBuyPrice  := na
    entrySellPrice := na
    entryBuyPower  := 0.0
    entrySellPower := 0.0
    int entryCursor = 0

    if showEntryInput and totalSwings > 0
        float bestBuyPrice  = na
        float bestSellPrice = na
        float bestBuyPower  = 0.0
        float bestSellPower = 0.0
        int   bestBuyLeft   = bar_index
        int   bestSellLeft  = bar_index
        // Searching every drawn swing, not just the newest: the strongest level
        // available is often in an older leg price has not returned to yet, and
        // an untested imbalance is more interesting than a tested one.
        int searchFrom = entrySourceInput == "Newest swing only" ? totalSwings - 1 : 0
        for i = searchFrom to totalSwings - 1
            SwingProfile p = i < swingCount ? array.get(swings, i) : liveSwing
            if not na(p)
                [bIdx, bPow, sIdx, sPow] = bestEntry(p, close, entryMinStrengthInput / 100)
                if bIdx >= 0 and bPow > bestBuyPower
                    bestBuyPower := bPow
                    bestBuyPrice := rowPrice(p, bIdx)
                    bestBuyLeft  := p.leftBar
                if sIdx >= 0 and sPow > bestSellPower
                    bestSellPower := sPow
                    bestSellPrice := rowPrice(p, sIdx)
                    bestSellLeft  := p.leftBar

        bool haveBuy  = not na(bestBuyPrice)
        bool haveSell = not na(bestSellPrice)
        // Best Side draws only the level the profile actually argues for.
        bool drawBuy = switch entryModeInput
            ENTRY_BUY  => haveBuy
            ENTRY_SELL => false
            ENTRY_BOTH => haveBuy
            => haveBuy and bestBuyPower >= bestSellPower
        bool drawSell = switch entryModeInput
            ENTRY_BUY  => false
            ENTRY_SELL => haveSell
            ENTRY_BOTH => haveSell
            => haveSell and bestSellPower > bestBuyPower
        // Published only when DRAWN. Publishing a level that is not on the chart
        // would let the alert fire for something the user cannot see, which is
        // the kind of inconsistency that makes an indicator untrustworthy.
        if drawBuy
            entryBuyPrice := bestBuyPrice
            entryBuyPower := bestBuyPower
            drawEntry(array.get(entryLines, entryCursor), array.get(entryTags, entryCursor),
                 bestBuyPrice, bestBuyPower, true, bestBuyLeft)
            entryCursor += 1
        if drawSell
            entrySellPrice := bestSellPrice
            entrySellPower := bestSellPower
            drawEntry(array.get(entryLines, entryCursor), array.get(entryTags, entryCursor),
                 bestSellPrice, bestSellPower, false, bestSellLeft)
            entryCursor += 1
    hideLinesFrom(entryLines, entryCursor)
    hideLabelsFrom(entryTags, entryCursor)

// ————— Visuals: summary table
var table dash = table.new(
     tablePosFrom(tablePosInput), 2, 19,
     bgcolor=getPanelColor(themeInput, 15), border_width=1,
     border_color=getPanelColor(themeInput, 100),
     frame_color=color.new(mutedColor, 70), frame_width=1)

if showTableInput and barstate.islast
    SwingProfile shown = na(liveSwing) ?
         (swingCount > 0 ? array.get(swings, swingCount - 1) : na) : liveSwing
    bool haveSwing = not na(shown)
    float buyPct = haveSwing and shown.totalVol > 0 ? shown.buyVol / shown.totalVol * 100 : na

    // The entry row first: it is the only line on the chart that answers a
    // question rather than describing history, so burying it under six
    // reference prices would be the wrong order.
    bool buyIsBest = not na(entryBuyPrice) and
         (na(entrySellPrice) or entryBuyPower >= entrySellPower)
    float shownEntry = buyIsBest ? entryBuyPrice : entrySellPrice
    float shownPower = buyIsBest ? entryBuyPower : entrySellPower
    color entryColor = buyIsBest ? buyColorInput : sellColorInput
    string entryText = na(shownEntry) ? "none" :
         (buyIsBest ? "BUY " : "SELL ") + str.tostring(shownEntry, format.mintick)

    // Two different numbers, and conflating them is what would mislead you:
    // rows is the resolution the profile was BUILT at, boxes is how much of the
    // drawing budget that resolution is currently spending.
    int shownRows = haveSwing ? array.size(shown.buyRows) : 0
    string rowNote = not haveSwing ? "--" :
         shownRows < rowsInput ?
         str.tostring(shownRows) + " / " + str.tostring(rowsInput) + " (tick limit)" :
         str.tostring(shownRows)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 0, "SWING PROFILE", text_color=textColor, text_size=size.normal,
         text_halign=text.align_center)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 0, "", text_color=textColor)
    table.merge_cells(dash, 0, 0, 1, 0)

    dashRow(dash, 1, "Entry level", entryText, mutedColor,
         na(shownEntry) ? mutedColor : entryColor)
    dashRow(dash, 2, "Strength", na(shownEntry) ? "--" :
         str.tostring(shownPower * 100, "#.#") + "%", mutedColor,
         na(shownEntry) ? mutedColor : entryColor)
    dashRow(dash, 3, "Distance", na(shownEntry) ? "--" :
         str.tostring(math.abs(close - shownEntry), format.mintick) + "  (" +
         str.tostring(math.abs(close - shownEntry) / close * 100, "#.##") + "%)",
         mutedColor, textColor)

    // Hit rate sits with the entry block because it is the only thing on the
    // panel that says whether the entry block has been worth anything.
    string hitText = not showStatsInput ? "off" :
         statResolved == 0 ? "no data yet" :
         str.tostring(statWins * 100.0 / statResolved, "#") + "%  (" +
         str.tostring(statResolved) + ")"
    dashRow(dash, 4, "Level hit rate", hitText, mutedColor,
         not showStatsInput or statResolved == 0 ? mutedColor :
         statResolved < 20 ? pocColorInput :
         statWins * 2 >= statResolved ? buyColorInput : sellColorInput)
    dashRow(dash, 5, "Absorption",
         absorptionState == "none" ? "--" : str.upper(absorptionState), mutedColor,
         absorptionState == "bullish" ? buyColorInput :
         absorptionState == "bearish" ? sellColorInput : mutedColor)
    dashRow(dash, 6, "Confluence zones",
         showConfluenceInput ? str.tostring(zoneCount) : "off", mutedColor,
         zoneCount > 0 ? confluenceColorInput : mutedColor)
    dashRow(dash, 7, "Naked POC", na(nakedPocPrice) ? "none" :
         str.tostring(nakedPocPrice, format.mintick), mutedColor,
         na(nakedPocPrice) ? mutedColor : pocColorInput)
    dashRow(dash, 8, "Value migration", valueMigration, mutedColor,
         valueMigration == "UP" ? buyColorInput :
         valueMigration == "DOWN" ? sellColorInput : mutedColor)
    // The live bar's own delta — the number the bubbles are made of. Shown
    // whether or not the bar qualifies, so a bar with NO bubble can be read
    // rather than merely noticed as absent.
    dashRow(dash, 9, "Bar delta",
         barVolTotal <= 0 ? "--" :
         (barDeltaPct >= 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(barDeltaPct * 100, "#.#") + "%  " +
         str.tostring(bubbleVolRatio, "#.#") + "x vol", mutedColor,
         strongBull ? bubbleBullColorInput : strongBear ? bubbleBearColorInput :
         nz(barDeltaPct) >= 0 ? buyColorInput : sellColorInput)
    dashRow(dash, 10, "Swing volume", haveSwing ? formatVolume(shown.totalVol) : "--",
         mutedColor, textColor)
    dashRow(dash, 11, "Buy share", na(buyPct) ? "--" : str.tostring(buyPct, "#.#") + "%",
         mutedColor, nz(buyPct, 50) >= 50 ? buyColorInput : sellColorInput)
    dashRow(dash, 12, "POC", haveSwing ? str.tostring(rowPrice(shown, shown.pocIdx), format.mintick) : "--",
         mutedColor, pocColorInput)
    dashRow(dash, 13, "Value area", haveSwing ?
         str.tostring(rowPrice(shown, shown.vaBotIdx), format.mintick) + " - " +
         str.tostring(rowPrice(shown, shown.vaTopIdx), format.mintick) : "--",
         mutedColor, vaColorInput)
    dashRow(dash, 14, "Max buy", haveSwing ?
         str.tostring(rowPrice(shown, shown.maxBuyIdx), format.mintick) : "--",
         mutedColor, maxBuyColorInput)
    dashRow(dash, 15, "Max sell", haveSwing ?
         str.tostring(rowPrice(shown, shown.maxSellIdx), format.mintick) : "--",
         mutedColor, maxSellColorInput)
    dashRow(dash, 16, "Rows / swing", rowNote, mutedColor,
         shownRows < rowsInput ? pocColorInput : textColor)
    // Provenance. A number whose source is invisible invites more trust than
    // it has earned, and "measured" and "modelled" are not the same claim.
    dashRow(dash, 17, "Data", not haveSwing ? "--" :
         not intrabarUsable ? "bar model" :
         str.tostring(shown.intrabarPct) + "% intrabar", mutedColor,
         not haveSwing or not intrabarUsable ? mutedColor :
         shown.intrabarPct >= 80 ? buyColorInput :
         shown.intrabarPct >= 30 ? pocColorInput : mutedColor)
    dashRow(dash, 18, "Boxes used",
         str.tostring(boxesUsed) + " / " + str.tostring(MAX_ROW_BOXES), mutedColor,
         boxesUsed >= MAX_ROW_BOXES ? pocColorInput : textColor)

// ————— Visuals: strength bubbles
//
// plotshape, not label.new. There is one of these per qualifying bar across the
// whole chart, and labels are capped at 500 — the oldest would start vanishing
// on any decent history, silently. plotshape has no such limit.
//
// `location` must be a compile-time constant, which is why the bear side needs
// two calls rather than one with a computed location. Each is inert when its
// position mode is off, so only one ever draws.
bool bearBelow = bubblePosInput == "Below bars"
plotshape(strongBull, title="Strong Bull Bubble", style=shape.circle,
     location=location.belowbar, color=bullBubbleColor, size=size.tiny,
     display=display.pane)
plotshape(strongBear and bearBelow, title="Strong Bear Bubble (below)",
     style=shape.circle, location=location.belowbar, color=bearBubbleColor,
     size=size.tiny, display=display.pane)
plotshape(strongBear and not bearBelow, title="Strong Bear Bubble (above)",
     style=shape.circle, location=location.abovebar, color=bearBubbleColor,
     size=size.tiny, display=display.pane)

// ————— Alerts
alertcondition(showAbsorptionInput and absorptionState == "bullish" and
     absorptionBar == bar_index - 1, "Bullish absorption",
     "A new swing low formed on RISING buy share - selling is being absorbed.")
alertcondition(showAbsorptionInput and absorptionState == "bearish" and
     absorptionBar == bar_index - 1, "Bearish absorption",
     "A new swing high formed on RISING sell share - buying is being absorbed.")
alertcondition(bubbleAlertInput and strongBull and barstate.isconfirmed,
     "Strong bullish bar",
     "A bar closed up with buying volume overwhelming selling on above-average volume.")
alertcondition(bubbleAlertInput and strongBear and barstate.isconfirmed,
     "Strong bearish bar",
     "A bar closed down with selling volume overwhelming buying on above-average volume.")
alertcondition(newSegment, "New swing confirmed",
     "A new swing has been confirmed (pivot, higher-timeframe pivot, or session close).")

// Gated on bar close, deliberately. Mid-bar a touch can un-happen, and an alert
// for something that never finished is worse than no alert at all.
if pocAlertInput and barstate.islast and barstate.isconfirmed and not na(nakedPocPrice)
    if low <= nakedPocPrice and high >= nakedPocPrice
        alert(levelAlertPayload("naked_poc", nakedPocPrice, alertFormatInput),
             alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

if entryAlertInput and barstate.islast and barstate.isconfirmed
    if not na(entryBuyPrice) and low <= entryBuyPrice and high >= entryBuyPrice
        alert(entryAlertPayload(true, entryBuyPrice, entryBuyPower, alertFormatInput),
             alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
    if not na(entrySellPrice) and low <= entrySellPrice and high >= entrySellPrice
        alert(entryAlertPayload(false, entrySellPrice, entrySellPower, alertFormatInput),
             alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

// ————— Debug / Test block (keep testModeInput false when handing off)
int TEST_COUNT = 15     // keep in sync with the assertFailed() calls below
var int passCount = 0
var int failCount = 0

// @function Draws a failure marker and RETURNS 1 on failure, 0 on pass. Pine
// forbids a function from modifying a global (CE10088), so the counting happens
// at the call site.
assertFailed(bool condition, string description) =>
    if not condition
        label.new(bar_index, high, "FAIL: " + description, color=sellColorInput,
             textcolor=color.white, style=label.style_label_down, size=size.small)
    condition ? 0 : 1

if testModeInput
    int fails = 0
    // 1. The stored swings must never exceed the configured cap.
    fails += assertFailed(array.size(swings) <= maxSwingsInput, "swing list exceeded its cap")
    // 2. Every marker index must be inside the row range.
    fails += assertFailed(
         na(liveSwing) or (liveSwing.pocIdx >= 0 and
         liveSwing.pocIdx < array.size(liveSwing.buyRows) and
         liveSwing.maxBuyIdx >= 0 and
         liveSwing.maxBuyIdx < array.size(liveSwing.buyRows) and
         liveSwing.maxSellIdx >= 0 and
         liveSwing.maxSellIdx < array.size(liveSwing.buyRows)),
         "marker row index out of range")
    // 3. Buying can never exceed the volume the swing actually traded.
    fails += assertFailed(
         na(liveSwing) or liveSwing.buyVol <= liveSwing.totalVol + 1,
         "buy volume exceeds total volume")
    // 4. A profile row must have positive height, and never be finer than a tick.
    fails += assertFailed(
         na(liveSwing) or liveSwing.step >= syminfo.mintick * 0.999,
         "profile row is thinner than one tick")
    // 5. The drawing must never exceed the box budget it was given.
    fails += assertFailed(boxesUsed <= MAX_ROW_BOXES, "row boxes exceeded the budget")
    // 6. The value area must contain the POC and stay inside the row range.
    fails += assertFailed(
         na(liveSwing) or (liveSwing.vaBotIdx <= liveSwing.pocIdx and
         liveSwing.vaTopIdx >= liveSwing.pocIdx and liveSwing.vaBotIdx >= 0 and
         liveSwing.vaTopIdx < array.size(liveSwing.buyRows)),
         "value area does not bracket the POC")
    // 7. A buy level must sit BELOW price and a sell level ABOVE it. Getting
    //    this backwards would be the worst failure available to this script:
    //    a level that looks authoritative and is on the wrong side.
    fails += assertFailed(
         (na(entryBuyPrice) or entryBuyPrice < close) and
         (na(entrySellPrice) or entrySellPrice > close),
         "entry level is on the wrong side of price")
    // 8. A drawn level must clear the strength floor it claims to respect.
    fails += assertFailed(
         (na(entryBuyPrice) or entryBuyPower >= entryMinStrengthInput / 100) and
         (na(entrySellPrice) or entrySellPower >= entryMinStrengthInput / 100),
         "entry level is below the minimum strength")
    // 9. Wins can never exceed resolved outcomes. A hit rate over 100% would be
    //    the most embarrassing number this table could print.
    fails += assertFailed(statWins <= statResolved and statWins >= 0,
         "hit rate counters are inconsistent")
    // 10. Confluence must stay inside the boxes reserved for it.
    fails += assertFailed(zoneCount <= array.size(zoneBoxes),
         "confluence drew more zones than it has boxes")
    // 11. Absorption is a closed set of three values; anything else means a
    //     typo has been silently rendering as an empty table cell.
    fails += assertFailed(
         absorptionState == "none" or absorptionState == "bullish" or
         absorptionState == "bearish", "absorption state is not a known value")
    // 12. Provenance is a percentage, and a percentage over 100 would mean the
    //     intrabar counter is counting bars that were not scanned.
    fails += assertFailed(
         na(liveSwing) or (liveSwing.intrabarPct >= 0 and liveSwing.intrabarPct <= 100),
         "intrabar coverage is not a valid percentage")
    // 13. Value migration is a closed set; anything else is a typo rendering as
    //     an empty cell.
    fails += assertFailed(
         valueMigration == "--" or valueMigration == "UP" or
         valueMigration == "DOWN" or valueMigration == "FLAT",
         "value migration is not a known value")
    // 14. Delta is a share of the bar's own volume, so it cannot leave -1..+1.
    //     Outside that range means buy and sell volume no longer sum to the
    //     total, which would corrupt every bubble and the profile with it.
    fails += assertFailed(barDeltaPct >= -1.0001 and barDeltaPct <= 1.0001,
         "bar delta is outside -100%..+100%")
    // 15. One bar cannot be both overwhelmingly bought and overwhelmingly sold.
    fails += assertFailed(not (strongBull and strongBear),
         "a bar is flagged strong in both directions")
    failCount += fails
    passCount += TEST_COUNT - fails

var table testTable = table.new(
     position.bottom_left, 2, 2, border_width=1,
     bgcolor=getPanelColor(themeInput, 15),
     frame_color=color.new(mutedColor, 70), frame_width=1)
if testModeInput and barstate.islast
    dashRow(testTable, 0, "Passed", str.tostring(passCount), mutedColor, buyColorInput)
    dashRow(testTable, 1, "Failed", str.tostring(failCount), mutedColor,
         failCount > 0 ? sellColorInput : buyColorInput)
